update public.posts set
  slug = 'stock-evaluation-criteria',
  title = 'My Stock Evaluation Criteria',
  excerpt = 'Before I buy any company, it has to pass a set of rules I wrote down in advance. Here is why every investor needs criteria like these, and the ones I use to stay calm when the market gets excited.',
  seo_description = 'The criteria I use to evaluate a stock before buying, and why fixed rules beat hype and fear of missing out.',
  content = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"Why Establish Investment Criteria?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"When investing in individual company stocks, it’s essential to have a clear set of criteria for evaluating a stock’s potential. These criteria act as guardrails, helping investors stay objective and avoid making emotional decisions. For example, during periods of hype around meme stocks or when FOMO (fear of missing out) is at its peak, pre-established guidelines offer a rational framework for decision-making. Additionally, regularly reviewing investments against these criteria can inform decisions about when to sell or when to revise outdated approaches.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"What Constitutes Investment Criteria?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Before outlining specific criteria, it’s important to define what qualifies. Investment criteria go beyond simple screening filters. For instance, a price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio under 20 is not sufficient on its own. Instead, criteria should represent broader concepts that are consistently applicable while remaining as specific as possible. Using the P/E ratio as an example, a more meaningful criterion might be that the stock is undervalued by at least 20% relative to its fair valuation, even if the method for determining fair value varies by company.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"My Investment Criteria","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Here is my personal list of investment criteria, along with the rationale behind each. These have evolved over time and will likely continue to adapt as I gain more experience. My criteria address both the company and its stock.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Net Income Positive","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"A company’s value is ultimately tied to its ability to generate cash. I prioritise companies that have demonstrated their capacity to produce positive returns. While this doesn’t guarantee scalability or sustainability, it shows the business model’s viability. Although this approach might miss out on early growth phases, I believe the risk/reward profile is generally better once this milestone is achieved. Valuation methods such as discounted cash flow (DCF) and earnings per share (EPS) rely on positive income; without it, substantial guesswork is required.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Attractive Valuation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Ideally, I aim to invest in exceptional companies at significant discounts. However, given current market conditions, this isn’t always possible. I consider investments that meet one of the following: a great company at a fair valuation or a fair company at a great valuation. While Warren Buffett famously said it’s better to buy a great company at a fair price than a fair company at a great price, the rapid pace of technological disruption has increasingly challenged even the strongest companies. Examples such as Blockbuster, General Motors, and Toys “R” Us illustrate this point. As such, I value both scenarios under the broader concept of “attractive valuation”.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Shareholder-Friendly Management","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"I look for companies that consistently return value to shareholders. This includes avoiding excessive share dilution, as it directly reduces the ownership proportion per share. Even if the overall share count doesn’t increase, high levels of stock-based compensation can indicate a company is using significant cash flow to offset this expense. While I recognise this may be necessary in competitive industries to retain talent, excessive dilution is a concern. On the positive side, buybacks and dividends are favourable, with consistent buybacks having an edge due to the flexibility they provide. Unlike dividends, which are taxed before reinvestment, buybacks increase the stock’s value, allowing investors to decide when to incur tax on sales.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Sector Diversification and Exclusions","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"This criterion is influenced by the composition of my broader portfolio. I believe it’s prudent to diversify across at least 3–4 sectors, so individual investments that enhance diversification are preferred over those that create excessive concentration in a single sector. This approach helps mitigate risks from unpredictable macroeconomic events, such as natural disasters or major legislative changes.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Clean Balance Sheet","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"A strong balance sheet ensures a company has the flexibility to adapt to sudden changes in its business environment. Highly leveraged companies face significant challenges in responding to new circumstances due to their debt burden. I prefer companies with a (total debt – cash) / EBITDA ratio of 3 or less, as this indicates manageable leverage.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}'::jsonb,
  cover_image_url = '/covers/posts/stock-criteria.webp',
  cover_image_alt = 'The word Criteria in large letters over a steadily rising line',
  tags = array['Valuation', 'Beginners']::text[],
  series = null,
  author = null,
  reading_minutes = 3
where slug = 'stock-evaluation-criteria';

update public.posts set
  slug = 'is-big-tech-really-this-dumb-deepseek',
  title = 'Is Big Tech Really This Dumb? How DeepSeek Built an AI for $6 Million',
  excerpt = 'A team of about 100 engineers built DeepSeek for a reported $6 million, while US tech giants have spent hundreds of billions. What that means for Big Tech''s moat, and why open-source AI could change who wins.',
  seo_description = 'DeepSeek''s $6 million AI and what it means for Big Tech: open-source models, AI costs and the end of the walled garden.',
  content = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"It’s the billion-dollar question on everyone’s mind:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Did US tech giants really spend ","type":"text"},{"text":"hundreds of billions","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" over several years just to build something that a small team of 100 Chinese engineers managed to create for a mere ","type":"text"},{"text":"$6 million","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":"?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/imported/is-big-tech-really-this-dumb-how-100-chinese-engineers-built-an-ai-for-6m-that/1.webp","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The shockwaves from this revelation are hitting hard, and the implications could be massive.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"Why the fear","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"At first glance, you might think:","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"“Okay, but how does this affect end users? Companies are still going to need AI services, right?”","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Not necessarily.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"DeepSeek isn’t necessarily ","type":"text"},{"text":"better","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" than OpenAI’s models, but it’s ","type":"text"},{"text":"just as good","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" for most tasks—and ","type":"text"},{"text":"far more accessible","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/imported/is-big-tech-really-this-dumb-how-100-chinese-engineers-built-an-ai-for-6m-that/2.webp","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Here’s the game-changer:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"It enables AI innovation everywhere.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Developers worldwide can collaborate, refine it, and improve it continuously—just like open-source software has done for decades.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Deepseek is free.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Anyone can take it, modify it, and run it independently—without paying OpenAI, Google, or any other AI giant.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"It gives total control to users.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" No reliance on centralised AI providers, no forced subscriptions, no restrictions on how you can use it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"The Stockfish Moment for AI","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"This is ","type":"text"},{"text":"Stockfish for AI","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/imported/is-big-tech-really-this-dumb-how-100-chinese-engineers-built-an-ai-for-6m-that/3.webp","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"If you’re into chess, you know what happened when Stockfish became the dominant engine: No matter how much Google’s AlphaZero tried to outpace it, the open-source community ","type":"text"},{"text":"always caught up","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":". And ultimately, Stockfish remained the go-to choice because it was free, continuously improving, and available to all.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"DeepSeek does the same for AI.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Now, startups in ","type":"text"},{"text":"every country","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" can ","type":"text"},{"text":"immediately","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" start from the current state-of-the-art instead of lagging years behind industry leaders.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Europe?","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" No more reliance on the US or pressure to comply with Big Tech’s rules. They can launch their own AI models while following EU regulations.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Developing countries?","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" No need for billion-dollar investments. They can build AI solutions at scale without buying proprietary models.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Businesses?","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" They no longer have to pay OpenAI or Google for AI services they can run in-house at a fraction of the cost.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"A Future Beyond Big Tech","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The bigger picture is clear:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"If a group of engineers can replicate years of Big Tech’s AI development ","type":"text"},{"text":"for just $6 million","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", what’s stopping others from doing the same?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The myth that only trillion-dollar companies can build world-class AI is crumbling.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"And if AI is truly democratised through open-source models like DeepSeek, the entire ","type":"text"},{"text":"business model of AI as a walled garden controlled by a handful of corporations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" could collapse.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Big Tech isn’t dumb—but they may have just lost their greatest competitive edge.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb,
  cover_image_url = '/imported/is-big-tech-really-this-dumb-how-100-chinese-engineers-built-an-ai-for-6m-that/1.webp',
  cover_image_alt = 'Is Big Tech Really This Dumb? How 100 Chinese Engineers Built an AI for $6M That Shakes the Industry',
  tags = array['AI', 'Technology']::text[],
  series = null,
  author = null,
  reading_minutes = 2
where slug = 'is-big-tech-really-this-dumb-how-100-chinese-engineers-built-an-ai-for-6m-that';

update public.posts set
  slug = 'novo-nordisk-overvalued-or-a-forgotten-gem',
  title = 'Novo Nordisk (NVO): Overvalued or a Forgotten Gem?',
  excerpt = 'Novo Nordisk earns an 85% gross margin, returns 30 to 60% on its capital and leads the boom in weight-loss drugs. It is not cheap at 29 times earnings, but its fundamentals go a long way towards justifying the price.',
  seo_description = 'Is Novo Nordisk worth its price? Margins, the weight-loss boom, competition from Eli Lilly and copycats, and the real risks.',
  content = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"stockSnapshot","attrs":{"company":"Novo Nordisk","ticker":"NVO","exchange":"NYSE","verdict":"watch","currency":"USD","summary":"Quality at a fair price. A top contender to keep an eye on.","metrics":[{"label":"Market cap","value":"~US$390B"},{"label":"P/E","value":"~29.5x"},{"label":"Gross margin","value":"85%"}],"price":"90.65"}},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"What Makes Novo Nordisk a Standout?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"🔹 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Incredible Profit Margins","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" – Gross margin at ","type":"text"},{"text":"85%","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", net margin at ","type":"text"},{"text":"35%","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"🔹 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Strong Returns on Investment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" – ROIC consistently between ","type":"text"},{"text":"30-60%","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"🔹 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Massive Market Potential","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" – Obesity and diabetes treatments are in high demand","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"🔹 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Growing Dividends","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" – A ","type":"text"},{"text":"21% boost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" this year alone","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"Imagine a business that consistently pulls in revenue, operates with extreme efficiency, and rewards its investors generously. That’s Novo Nordisk (NVO) in a nutshell. Despite having a market cap that rivals Denmark’s GDP, Novo Nordisk still has room to expand, particularly in the obesity sector. Here’s why it remains an exciting investment.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"The Weight Loss Revolution","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Most people hit the gym for ","type":"text"},{"text":"looks first, health second","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":"—that’s human nature. Weight-loss drugs like Ozempic and Wegovy tap into this reality, offering both aesthetic and health benefits. This means continuous demand and ","type":"text"},{"text":"repeat customers","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"text":"Novo’s Blockbuster Medications","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Novo is a leader in ","type":"text"},{"text":"diabetes and weight-loss","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" solutions, with semaglutide as its flagship product:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"✅ ","type":"text"},{"text":"Ozempic","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" – Manages diabetes","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"✅ ","type":"text"},{"text":"Wegovy","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" – Designed for weight loss","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"✅ ","type":"text"},{"text":"Rybelsus","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" – Oral semaglutide alternative","type":"text"}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"The Competitive Landscape","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"With such a blockbuster drug, competition was inevitable.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"text":"Copycat Manufacturers","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Some companies, like ","type":"text"},{"text":"Hims & Hers (HIMS)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", exploited loopholes to produce GLP-1 medications while supply was tight. However, with the FDA lifting the “shortage” designation, these copycats will soon be out of the picture. Novo is also boosting production with ","type":"text"},{"text":"new manufacturing plants","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" in Italy, the U.S., and Belgium.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"text":"Novo vs. Eli Lilly","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Eli Lilly’s ","type":"text"},{"text":"Tirzepatide (Monjaro)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" is a strong competitor, but here’s the breakdown:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"🔸 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Tirzepatide:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Slightly better for weight loss & certain heart conditions","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"🔸 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Semaglutide:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" More comprehensive cardiovascular benefits","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Both companies will thrive, but Novo has the ","type":"text"},{"text":"first-mover advantage","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":"—Ozempic is already a ","type":"text"},{"text":"household name","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"Is Novo a Smart Investment?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"💰 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Market Cap:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" ~$390B","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"💰 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Enterprise Value (EV):","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" ~$413B (after liabilities)","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"💰 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Net Profit:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" ~$14B, growing ","type":"text"},{"text":"20% per year","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"💰 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Price-to-Earnings (P/E) Ratio:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" ~29.5x","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"While Novo isn’t trading at a bargain, its ","type":"text"},{"text":"steady growth and market leadership","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" make it worth the price. By comparison, Eli Lilly’s P/E ratio is ","type":"text"},{"text":"75x","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", making Novo look much more reasonable.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"📢 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Quote:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" “It’s better to own a great company at a fair price than a fair company at a great price.” – Warren Buffett","type":"text"}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"Addressing Common Concerns","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"text":"1. U.S. Tariffs on Pharmaceuticals?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"🚫 Not a major threat—Novo is ramping up ","type":"text"},{"text":"U.S. production","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" to stay ahead.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"text":"2. What About Patent Expiry?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"🚫 Generic versions will arrive in the ","type":"text"},{"text":"2030s","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", but Novo is already working on ","type":"text"},{"text":"next-gen treatments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" like Cagrisema and Amycretin.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"Real Risks to Watch","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"⚠ ","type":"text"},{"text":"Medicare Pricing Negotiations (2027):","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Could lower prices, but demand may compensate.","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"⚠ ","type":"text"},{"text":"China Patent Expiry (2026):","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Novo is fighting for an extension.","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"⚠ ","type":"text"},{"text":"Insider Buying Slowdown:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" No major insider purchases recently.","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"⚠ ","type":"text"},{"text":"Potential New Competitors:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Unlikely to pose a serious challenge in the near future.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"Final Thoughts","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Novo Nordisk is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"profit machine","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" with a growing market, unbeatable margins, and a solid financial base. While it’s not dirt cheap, its ","type":"text"},{"text":"fundamentals justify the price","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":". If you’re looking for a ","type":"text"},{"text":"long-term pharmaceutical investment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", Novo is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"top contender that I would keep my eyes on.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}'::jsonb,
  cover_image_url = '/covers/posts/novo-nordisk.webp',
  cover_image_alt = 'NVO in large letters over a price line that rises and levels off',
  tags = array['Healthcare', 'Stock Market']::text[],
  series = null,
  author = null,
  reading_minutes = 2
where slug = 'novo-nordisk-overvalued-or-a-forgotten-gem';

update public.posts set
  slug = 'best-stock-analysis-tools-for-2025',
  title = 'The Best Stock Analysis Tools for 2025',
  excerpt = 'Five research platforms compared, from GuruFocus to Koyfin: what each does well, where each falls short, and the one I would pick for investors who want a tool that fits their own style.',
  seo_description = 'GuruFocus, PEvaluator, FinChat, Simply Wall St and Koyfin compared: the best stock analysis tools for 2025.',
  content = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Investing in stocks can feel daunting with an overload of data, making it tough to figure out what really counts. That’s why stock analysis tools are essential—they provide the insights you need to make more informed decisions. As we step into 2025, I’ve compiled a list of the top stock analysis tools designed to help you navigate the clutter. We’ll explore each tool’s advantages and drawbacks, and I’ll wrap up with my personal favourite. Let’s get started!","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/imported/best-stock-analysis-tools-for-2025/1.webp","alt":"A stock analysis dashboard showing financial strength, profitability and valuation scores","caption":""}},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"Key Attributes of an Outstanding Stock Analysis Tool","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Before diving into the lineup, here’s what I considered:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"• Data Accuracy – Reliable information is essential.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"• Robust Features – The more functionalities available, the better.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"• User-Friendly Interface – A simple, intuitive design saves time.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"• Cost Efficiency – Free or affordable options are a plus.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Every tool has its unique advantages, so let’s explore how they stack up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"#1 GuruFocus","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/imported/best-stock-analysis-tools-for-2025/1.webp","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"A Data-Heavy Powerhouse","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"If you love delving into details, GuruFocus is tailored for you. This platform brims with diverse valuation methods, including discounted cash flow (DCF), P/E ratios, and price-to-book metrics. It also supplies a wealth of historical data and fosters an active community for idea sharing. However, take note that some of its premium features are accessible only through costly subscriptions, and the interface might be a bit complex for beginners. Still, for the avid, detail-oriented investor, it’s a veritable treasure trove.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Pros:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Comprehensive valuation tools","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Moat rating","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Extensive historical data","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Active community","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cons:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Premium features come at an extra cost","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Not particularly beginner-friendly","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Subscriptions range from $500 to $2400 per year","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"#2 PEvaluator","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/imported/best-stock-analysis-tools-for-2025/2.webp","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Take Command of Your Analysis","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Looking for a tool that puts you in the driver’s seat? PEvaluator lets you build custom market models tailored to your investment approach, delivering fair price estimates on thousands of stocks. It offers features such as P/E analysis, moat ratings, comprehensive stock comparisons, portfolio tracking, and price alerts—all designed to empower smarter, more efficient investing.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Pros:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Intuitive interface","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Customisable market models with intrinsic value estimates","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Wide array of analytical tools","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Extensive historical financial data—all for just $10/month","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cons:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• The free tier is restricted to mega-cap stocks only","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"#3 FinChat","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/imported/best-stock-analysis-tools-for-2025/3.webp","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Your Conversational Stock Assistant","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Imagine having an AI companion to break down stocks for you—that’s FinChat. This tool blends intelligent, AI-driven insights with robust financial data, enabling you to screen stocks based on over 300 metrics or simply ask questions like you would to a financial expert. It’s designed with ease-of-use in mind and even offers a free tier for starters. If you appreciate tech-forward solutions and desire rapid, interactive answers, FinChat could be an excellent choice.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Pros:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• AI-powered insights","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• User-friendly interface","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Free tier available","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Access to a global stock database","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cons:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Relatively new and less established","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• May not offer the depth of analysis found in some legacy tools","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• AI-generated responses require verification","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Optimal use requires an upgrade to the $80/month tier","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"#4 Simply Wall St","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/imported/best-stock-analysis-tools-for-2025/4.webp","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The Visually Appealing Choice","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"If you’re new to investing, Simply Wall St might be right up your alley. It simplifies intricate valuation reports into clear, readable insights using a straightforward DCF model, and its free basic features are perfect for beginners. Plus, with a mobile app to monitor stocks on the go, it keeps you connected wherever you are. However, customisation is quite limited, and sometimes the data isn’t as up-to-date as you’d like. Overall, it’s an excellent option if you value simplicity and clarity over extensive detail.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Pros:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Easy-to-understand reports","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Free basic features","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Clean, intuitive design","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Affordable at $20/month","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cons:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Limited customisation options","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Occasional data lag","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"text":"#5 Koyfin","type":"text"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/imported/best-stock-analysis-tools-for-2025/5.webp","caption":""}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"A Graph-Heavy Power Tool for Investors","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Koyfin operates like a Swiss Army knife for those who crave data. It offers advanced charting, fully customizable dashboards, and a screener featuring over 500 metrics—including both fundamental and technical indicators. The free version is impressively robust, and you can even download your analyses for further exploration. On the flip side, it has a learning curve, and serious users might find the free plan a bit limiting. It’s a great choice for data enthusiasts who don’t mind a bit of initial setup. Popular among day traders who do more chart analysis work as well.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Pros:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Extensive metrics","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• High-quality charts","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Generous free tier","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Ability to download data","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Cons:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Requires time to master","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Performance can be sluggish at times","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Interface might feel cluttered","type":"text"},{"type":"hardBreak"},{"text":"• Subscription plans range from $50 to $200 per month","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Choosing the best stock analysis tool really depends on your specific needs. If you love diving deep into the details, GuruFocus is ideal. For a more straightforward experience, Simply Wall St delivers clarity, while FinChat offers a modern twist with its AI-powered insights. Koyfin, on the other hand, is perfect for those who thrive on rich, data-driven visuals. However, if you’re looking for a tool that adapts to your personal investing style, PEvaluator stands out as my top pick. Give it a try and share your feedback—happy investing!","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb,
  cover_image_url = '/covers/posts/analysis-tools.webp',
  cover_image_alt = 'The word Tools in large letters over a rising line',
  tags = array['Tools', 'Beginners']::text[],
  series = null,
  author = null,
  reading_minutes = 4
where slug = 'best-stock-analysis-tools-for-2025';

